{
  "id": "Compound::DB01236 - Gene::2557",
  "verbalization": "Sevoflurane is implicated in the interaction with Gabra4 through a complex network of molecular and cellular mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Sevoflurane binds to Gabrg3, which is a subunit of the Gaba-a receptor, and this binding is linked to the participation of Gabrg3 in Gaba-a receptor activity. This activity is then participated by Gabra4, indicating a potential functional relationship between Sevoflurane and Gabra4. Similarly, Sevoflurane also binds to Gabra6, which participates in benzodiazepine receptor activity, which is also participated by Gabra4. These findings suggest that Sevoflurane may influence Gabra4 through different receptor pathways, possibly by modulating the activity of Gaba-a and benzodiazepine receptors. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Gabra4 in mediating the effects of Sevoflurane, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
